00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.780
How do we resolve
these bottlenecks?

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.530
We're really proud that
they picked Google Cloud.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.410
So one of the next GPUs
that we just launched,

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:08.990
the NVIDIA Turing T4.

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.520
We just announced this week
that we're GA on the product.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:13.840
We're the first major
cloud to offer it.

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.190
This GPU is really
about scale out

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:21.060
workloads, both ML training, ML
inference, HPC, visualization,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:21.800
right?

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.250
It's only a 75 watt card.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.040
So what that means
is you realize

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.475
it's not about scaling
up in the terms of I

00:12:30.475 --> 00:12:31.850
have one workload,
I have one VM.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:34.220
I want a large workload.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:36.680
I want to be able to
replicate that out

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:39.020
in scale by going horizontal.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:42.200
It has a bunch of unique
features that we'll dig into.

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:48.300
First is the new types of
technology into the chip,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:48.800
right?

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.380
Compared to Pascal
versus Turing,

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:52.470
they've added two things.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.460
One, the same Tensor
cores that are

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:57.490
in v100 for mixed
precision training, right,

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.880
are also in Turing.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:00.890
But they've gone further, right?

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:02.048
And made it more flexible.

00:13:02.048 --> 00:13:03.590
And the second change
in the hardware

00:13:03.590 --> 00:13:06.920
is RT cores, which are great
for real time ray tracing

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.690
support for visualization.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.120
So great innovation by NVIDIA.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.970
Based on that RT cores
in real time ray tracing,

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:16.340
we've partnered
with NVIDIA to bring

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.200
Quadro virtual
workstations to Google

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:20.210
Cloud in the marketplace.

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:23.090
That is, once you tie in your
ML and HPC workload perhaps

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:27.080
and you want a visualization
component, create a VM.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.050
Turn on real time ray tracing
for optimal performance in 3D

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:32.180
rendering and be able
to show your results.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:39.510
Why is multi-precision helpful
and about access to compute?

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:42.340
So this is an illustration
about the Tensor cores, right?

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.110
And since GPU
compute is all about

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:49.080
offloading math, for the
first time ever in a new way,

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.160
the T4 GPU offers
the ability to choose

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.110
the amount of
performance you want

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:57.660
with choosing the level
of precision you want.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.820
So for HPC workloads, right, you
might want as high a precision

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.380
as possible.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:06.200
But maybe for ML
inference, it turns out

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:08.660
you can lower precision down
into eight or maybe even

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:10.310
into four, right?

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.440
And it doesn't change
your model's accuracy

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:13.580
with predictions.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:16.020
But you get a bunch
more performance.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.670
So for the first time ever
in CUDA, or in TensorFlow,

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.530
or in your ML
frameworks, you could

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.100
choose which level of
performance you want

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:27.240
and trade off based on accuracy.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:29.240
So that tradeoff-- it's great.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.610
You get great performance,
great pricing.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:34.190
Then cost of that, you
could do more innovation

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:37.698
by just doing ML and HPC over
and over again to innovate.

00:14:37.698 --> 00:14:38.990
But maybe it's hard to do that.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:42.440
Maybe your researchers, your
developers, your engineers,

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:44.000
maybe they're
focusing on customer

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.670
features over that
optimization performance.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:50.270
But NVIDIA just announced
something called AMP, Automatic

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:54.980
Mixed Precision, where some
ML frameworks, like MXNet

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.050
and TensorFlow, you
don't have to do

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.270
that optimization yourself.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.480
Just add two lines
of code, right?

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.850
And NVIDIA goes and
automatically optimizes

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:04.350
your model.

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:10.130
So our T4 offering that we
just did a GA announcement

00:15:10.130 --> 00:15:12.730
this week, it's available
in eight regions.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:16.600
Pricing is incredibly
attractive-- $0.95 an hour,

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:21.130
but as low as $0.29 an
hour for preemptible VMs.

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:26.780
So let's look at ML inference a
little bit, and on this T4 GPU.

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:28.220
So why ML inference on the T4?

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.000
First, that mixed precision.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.500
Right?

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:32.900
Looking at the performance
of the various generations

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:36.230
of GPUs, you'll see that
the amount of teraflops

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.240
per second increases
dramatically

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:41.120
for the next
generation of T4 GPU.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:45.760
And depending on what level
of performance you're getting,

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.000
you're going to pick that
precision that matters.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.460
Second, latency, right?

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:51.830
With CPU inference,
latency is measures

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:53.940
in the Tens of milliseconds.

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.330
But low latency is really
important for real time

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:57.770
inference workloads, right?

00:15:57.770 --> 00:16:02.190
When your end users using the
application, you want that.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.730
So T4 is purposely
built for this, right?

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:08.480
Let's look at some stats on
end user workloads, right?

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.160
Well, no matter
what your model is,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:11.930
you're going to see
speed-ups, right?

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:17.510
With video inference,
language, image recognition,

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:21.650
5x, 3x speed improvements
over the previous GPUs.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:25.240
And if you're comparing
for CPUs, it's even more.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:27.460
So we talked about the
need for low latency.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:30.030
T4 inference as low
as a millisecond,

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.860
for real time latency
and low batches.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:34.840
We've talked about
the eight regions

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:37.080
where T4's are available.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.400
And the second thing or the
third thing is our network.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.760
So now if you're a
social media company,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.870
or you have a
worldwide group, or you

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:50.860
have users in a certain area,
you really want low latency.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.590
But to get low latency,
it's not just the hardware.

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.502
You need global
distribution of sites.

00:16:56.502 --> 00:16:57.460
And it's not just that.

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:59.830
You also need a
world class network

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:01.870
that's built to give
Google search, right,

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.829
to your mobile phone or to
your browser really quickly.

00:17:05.829 --> 00:17:11.319
So altogether, that's a
great ML inference system.

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:13.410
But it's not just the hardware.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:15.380
It's not just the performance.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.069
It's also about the software.

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:20.230
So TensorRT is this piece
of software from NVIDIA

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.530
that optimizes
inference on GPUs.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:25.329
And some of their
new inference server

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:29.122
software, some really
great innovation as well.

00:17:29.122 --> 00:17:30.580
The thing I really
want to call out

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:32.090
here out of all these things--

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:33.790
the concurrent models.

00:17:33.790 --> 00:17:35.860
What that means is you
can run multiple models

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:38.200
at the same time and
doing inference, right,

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.980
in an aggregated way to really
use memory and processing

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:43.510
power of the GPU.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:45.190
The way I think of
it is, in essence,

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:47.810
multi-tenancy for inference.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:51.660
And there's a bunch of other
things that it comes along to.

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:55.550
So I think let's take a look
at a real world example, right?

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:58.800
There is a detailed blog that
we wrote that's posted up

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.290
that walks through this.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:02.790
But what it is, is let's say
you want to run a production ML

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:07.110
inference system, right, on
an NVIDIA GPU doing real time

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.500
inference serving.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.800
You might have your users
coming to a cloud load balancer.

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:17.060
That cloud load balancer will
send traffic to multiple zones.

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:19.910
Those zones will have what
we call managed instance

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:23.960
groups that are collections of
servers with infrastructure.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:26.090
Those managed instance
groups will have one or more

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:28.940
VMs depending on how
much load there is.

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:31.520
We'll do automatic scaling
based on utilization

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:33.500
of the infrastructure.

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:37.550
We'll use T4 GPUs with
a deep learning VM.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.140
So when you create the VM,
you pick deep learning.

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.640
You have all the stuff you
need right out the bat.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.860
We'll use object storage to
store our pre-trained model.

00:18:45.860 --> 00:18:48.440
When the VM boots up, it
just downloads the model.

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:50.390
And we'll use
Stackdriver, right,

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:53.690
to see the visualization of
how much traffic's coming in,

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:56.990
get the stats on CPU,
memory, and GPU utilization,

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:59.216
and do auto scaling
based on that.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:04.250
So for all of those
benefits, right,

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:07.190
Snapchat shows T4
for inference, right?

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:12.020
Snapchat makes a lot of money,
and ads are a big part of that.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:14.360
So they've taken their
monetization algorithm

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:15.830
from CPU.

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:17.760
They've partnered
with Google NVIDIA.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:21.470
They've optimized how to get
the best performance, as well

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.720
as the best cost savings.

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:24.900
And they picked T4.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.150
And it's not just the T4.

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:29.180
It's the fact that we're in
eight regions, Google Cloud's

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:33.580
network, worldwide
great inference.

00:19:33.580 --> 00:19:34.880
So that was one example.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.380
So let's look more broadly about
managing GPU infrastructure

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.850
and what you might
be able to do.

00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:41.925
So it's really choose
your own adventure.

00:19:41.925 --> 00:19:43.425
I remember when I
was a kid, I would

00:19:43.425 --> 00:19:45.280
read those choose your
own adventure books.

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:47.040
And if you're a
Netflix subscriber,

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.440
there's this really cool choose
your own adventure movie.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.330
People love it or hate it.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.250
But with infrastructure
and GPUs,

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:55.650
really, there's a choose
your own adventure game.

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:57.690
There's three kind of
basic models, right?

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:00.480
One, you could say,
I don't want to worry

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:01.920
about the infrastructure.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:05.580
I'm a developer, and I want to
use developer services or APIs,

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:06.080
right?

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.250
It's great in the sense if
you have an application,

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:11.460
and you innovate quickly
with hardware acceleration,

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:14.190
just talk to some API.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:15.870
Then there's managed services.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:18.270
This is I do have
data scientists.

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:20.940
I do have HPC researchers
and scientists.

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:24.220
I do want to worry about
my domain expertise.

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:27.980
But I want Google Cloud to
worry about the infrastructure.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:30.680
And then there's the green side,
the infrastructures as service.

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.530
These are VMs or containers.

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:34.280
Just, we're giving you
the infrastructure,

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:36.322
and you might be managing
your own infrastructure

00:20:36.322 --> 00:20:37.490
with your own code.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:40.280
The benefit of this is
maybe there's more cost

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.620
from an operations
standpoint about dealing

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:43.820
with that infrastructure.

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:46.790
But you gain things like
portability, control

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:48.590
or performance optimizations.

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:50.150
You could lift and
shift hardware.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:53.370
You could support open source.

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:56.540
Google Cloud had a lot of
announcements about openness,

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:58.450
hybrid, multi-cloud.

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:01.710
And by being infrastructures of
service, right, you gain those.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:04.970
And Google Cloud with GPUs, we
have access of all of these.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:08.840
On the API services, we
have ML services for text

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:12.350
to speech recognition, video
intelligence, translation,

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:13.440
vision.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.370
All of that is
hardware accelerated.

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:17.870
Our managed services, we have
Cloud Machine Learning Engine,

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:20.300
right, Hadoop as a
service, things like that,

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:22.830
and of course,
infrastructure as a service.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:25.760
So it's just a matter of what
your organization wants to do,

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.620
how you want to manage
it, and where you

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:30.705
see your value add as being.

00:21:30.705 --> 00:21:32.330
I'm not going to talk
a little bit more

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:33.483
about the API services.

00:21:33.483 --> 00:21:35.066
Because if you're a
developer, there's

00:21:35.066 --> 00:21:37.280
some other talks for
that here at Next.

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:38.780
But let's talk a
little bit briefly

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:41.020
about the managed services.

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:42.920
First is Cloud Machine
Learning Engine.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:43.420
Right?

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.470
It's all about you're
a data scientist.

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:46.780
You're a ML engineer.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:51.535
You want to create models, and
it's pick your own open source

00:21:51.535 --> 00:21:52.660
machine learning framework.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:54.610
And we'll do training for you.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:56.357
Think of it as a
bunch of training jobs

00:21:56.357 --> 00:21:57.565
with results and performance.

00:21:57.565 --> 00:22:00.272
And we help you optimize
and get better and better.

00:22:00.272 --> 00:22:02.730
But you don't have to worry
about the infrastructure of VMs

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:04.240
or containers.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:07.630
Also, we're launching
inference on GPUs as an alpha.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:08.230
Right?

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:11.260
So you can do batch inference,
right, or real time inference

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:12.670
workloads.

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:14.620
I really, really,
really like AutoML.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:15.500
This is great.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:16.000
Right?

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.270
Auto MLs-- if you're not
necessarily an organization

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.390
that has machine learning
scientist expertise,

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:25.150
but you have a bunch of data,
you have a bunch of developers,

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.390
and you want to
create an AI model,

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:31.150
right, without that,
auto ML is for you.

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:34.690
Hadoop and Spark has a
service with GPU powered

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:36.130
infrastructure, right?

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.650
Or ZYNC, which is video
rendering a service.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:43.373
All of it hardware acceleration,
all of it on GPUs, and those

00:22:43.373 --> 00:22:45.040
managed services you
don't have to worry

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:46.770
about the infrastructure.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.270
Let's talk a little bit more
about the actual infrastructure

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:50.620
services.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:52.270
GCE, obviously, right?

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:55.390
We have GPUs, API,
user interface,

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:57.887
as well as G Cloud utility.

00:22:57.887 --> 00:22:59.470
We have managed
instance groups, which

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:02.500
are those logical groups that
compute with auto scaling

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:06.190
and GPU statistics for
how busy your GPUs are,

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:10.050
and you can auto
scale based on that.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:13.410
This is the screenshot of our
user interface just showcasing

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:14.910
the flexibility.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:16.907
You'll notice here
there's little sliders.

00:23:16.907 --> 00:23:17.490
Pick them out.

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:18.540
Pick the CPUs.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.070
Pick the memory.

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:22.410
In the bottom right here,
which is kind of cut off

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:25.500
unfortunately, you get to
pick your own GPU type,

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:32.010
GPU count, complete flexibility
available on containers or VMs.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:35.130
Let's dive into the managed
interest groups a little bit.

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:36.780
Managed interest
groups are great tools

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:39.870
on our VM infrastructure
for batch workloads

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:44.110
or for real time
production workloads.

00:23:44.110 --> 00:23:47.557
So if you're doing training or
an HPC run, really typically,

00:23:47.557 --> 00:23:49.140
you'll have a bunch
of infrastructure.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:51.460
You'll have a job when
